{
  "project": {
    "title": "Country Economic Development Segmentation Using Macroeconomic Indicators",
    "domain": "Economics / Development Economics",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "prerequisites": [
      "Python basics",
      "pandas",
      "numpy",
      "scikit-learn",
      "matplotlib",
      "seaborn"
    ],
    "objective": "Segment countries into homogeneous economic groups based on macroeconomic and human-development indicators using K-Means clustering, and compare the data-driven segments against traditional classifications such as World Bank income groups.",
    "dataset": {
      "name": "Gapminder / World Bank Country Indicators",
      "source_url": "https://www.gapminder.org/data/",
      "alternative": "World Bank Open Data API: https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/all/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.PP.CD?format=json&date=2022",
      "file_name": "country_indicators.csv",
      "columns": [
        { "feature": "Country", "type": "string", "description": "Country name", "example": "Norway" },
        { "feature": "Region", "type": "string", "description": "Geographic region", "example": "Europe & Central Asia" },
        { "feature": "GDP_per_capita_PPP", "type": "float", "description": "Gross domestic product per capita, purchasing power parity (current international $)", "example": "82437.0" },
        { "feature": "LifeExpectancy", "type": "float", "description": "Average life expectancy at birth (years)", "example": "83.2" },
        { "feature": "FertilityRate", "type": "float", "description": "Births per woman", "example": "1.5" },
        { "feature": "Inflation", "type": "float", "description": "Annual consumer price inflation (%)", "example": "3.4" },
        { "feature": "Unemployment", "type": "float", "description": "Unemployment rate, total (% of labor force)", "example": "5.2" },
        { "feature": "TradeOpenness", "type": "float", "description": " Trade as a share of GDP ((exports+imports)/GDP %)", "example": "71.8" },
        { "feature": "GiniIndex", "type": "float", "description": "Income inequality (Gini coefficient, 0-100)", "example": "27.7" },
        { "feature": "GovEducationSpend", "type": "float", "description": "Government expenditure on education (% of GDP)", "example": "6.9" },
        { "feature": "CO2_per_capita", "type": "float", "description": "Carbon dioxide emissions per capita (tonnes)", "example": "7.6" }
      ],
      "notes": "Merge indicators from the same reference year (e.g., 2022). Where a value is missing for the exact year, take the most recent prior observation available and record the reference year in a separate column."
    },
    "tasks": [
      {
        "task_id": 1,
        "title": "Data Loading and Cleaning",
        "instructions": [
          "Load the CSV with pd.read_csv into a DataFrame named econ.",
          "Inspect with info(), describe(), and isna().sum(); report how many countries and what fraction of each indicator is missing.",
          "Drop rows where the essential identifiers or more than half of the indicators are missing (keep countries with a usable profile, e.g., dropna(thresh=...)).",
          "For the remaining missing numeric values, impute with the median per column: fillna(df[col].median()). Median is preferred over mean because GDP per capita and CO2 per capita are heavily right-skewed.",
          "Keep the Country and Region columns aside (they are metadata, not clustering features)."
        ],
        "expected_code": "import pandas as pd\n\necon = pd.read_csv('country_indicators.csv')\nprint(econ.shape, econ.isna().sum())\necon = econ.dropna(thresh=econ.shape[1] - 3)\nnum_cols = ['GDP_per_capita_PPP','LifeExpectancy','FertilityRate','Inflation','Unemployment','TradeOpenness','GiniIndex','GovEducationSpend','CO2_per_capita']\necon[num_cols] = econ[num_cols].fillna(econ[num_cols].median())",
        "hints": [
          "Counting missing data BEFORE dropping is itself a finding: financial-center microstates and conflict states are usually the poorly-covered rows.",
          "Do not impute before reporting missingness — silently patched data biases downstream conclusions."
        ]
      },
      {
        "task_id": 2,
        "title": "Feature Engineering",
        "instructions": [
          "Compute a crude composite 'human development proxy': the mean of standardized LifeExpectancy and the inverse of FertilityRate is one option; simpler is to simply keep both raw and let the model combine them.",
          "Compute GDP growth-adjusted openness if multiple years are available: TradeOpenness is already a ratio, so no division by GDP is needed.",
          "Winsorize extreme outliers (e.g., inflation above 300% or CO2 above 40 t/capita) at the 1st and 99th percentile to prevent a handful of hyperinflation/oil-state cases from distorting distances: use scipy.stats.mstats.winsorize or manual clipping.",
          "Name the resulting feature matrix X_raw with Country and Region preserved as separate Series country_names and regions for later labeling."
        ],
        "hints": [
          "Winsorizing is the standard compromise in cross-country regression/clustering: you lose information about extremes but keep them from dominating Euclidean distances.",
          "Arguably, extreme values (Venezuela's inflation) are the economically interesting story — winsorizing is a modeling choice you must state explicitly in the report."
        ]
      },
      {
        "task_id": 3,
        "title": "Log Transformation and Scaling",
        "instructions": [
          "Apply np.log1p to right-skewed variables only: GDP_per_capita_PPP, CO2_per_capita, and (if not winsorized) Inflation. Leave bounded/negative-capable variables (GiniIndex, Unemployment, LifeExpectancy, FertilityRate) unlogged — check each with a histogram or .skew().",
          "Standardize ALL features with StandardScaler into X_scaled so each contributes equally to Euclidean distance.",
          "Verify: X_scaled.mean(axis=0) approx 0 and std approx 1; print the skewness before and after transformation per column."
        ],
        "hints": [
          "Unlike the retail RFM case, here the decision of WHICH columns to log-transform is part of the analysis — justify each choice with a skewness statistic.",
          "Applying log1p to Gini or Unemployment is harmless but pointless; documenting why is good practice."
        ]
      },
      {
        "task_id": 4,
        "title": "Elbow Method and K-Means Clustering",
        "instructions": [
          "Loop k from 1 to 10 on X_scaled with KMeans(random_state=42, n_init=10), collecting inertia.",
          "Plot the elbow curve with labeled axes and a title.",
          "Additionally compute the silhouette score (sklearn.metrics.silhouette_score) for k=2..10 — with ~180 observations this is cheap and provides a non-visual second opinion on k.",
          "Choose k (typically 3-5 for country data; k=4 often mirrors a development-tier structure). Refit and store labels as econ['Cluster']."
        ],
        "hints": [
          "With small N (~180 countries), each cluster shift moves a meaningful number of countries — sensitivity analysis at k=3,4,5 is more informative here than in retail data.",
          "Expected qualitative outcome: a 'developed/aging', an 'emerging/middle-income', and a 'low-income/young' cluster, plus possibly an oil-rich or trade-hub outlier cluster."
        ]
      },
      {
        "task_id": 5,
        "title": "PCA Visualization",
        "instructions": [
          "Fit PCA(n_components=2) on X_scaled and build pca_df with columns PC1, PC2, Cluster, Country, Region.",
          "Plot with seaborn scatterplot, hue='Cluster'; annotate a handful of landmark countries with ax.annotate to anchor interpretation (e.g., USA, Norway, Niger, Singapore).",
          "Print pca.explained_variance_ratio_ and the loadings matrix (features x PC1/PC2) to interpret the axes economically."
        ],
        "hints": [
          "PC1 almost always captures the overall development gradient (GDP, life expectancy, CO2 loading together, fertility opposite). You can then honestly label PC1 'level of development' and check PC2 for a secondary structure such as inequality or openness.",
          "Annotating countries turns an abstract scatter into an immediately readable economic map."
        ]
      },
      {
        "task_id": 6,
        "title": "Interpretation and Validation Against Known Classifications",
        "instructions": [
          "Group by Cluster and report mean/median of each indicator in original units (un-transform).",
          "Name each cluster based on its profile (e.g., 'advanced aging economies', 'resource-dependent middle income', 'young low-income').",
          "Cross-tabulate Cluster against Region and against World Bank income group if available: pd.crosstab(econ['Cluster'], regions, normalize='index') — a high diagonal concentration means your clustering recovered known geography/income structure.",
          "Identify the most 'misclassified-feeling' countries: e.g., developed economies landing in the middle cluster. These anomalies are the analytical payoff of unsupervised methods."
        ],
        "hints": [
          "The deliverable of this project is the anomaly table, not the clusters themselves: countries whose multidimensional profile differs from their income-group peers (e.g., high-GDP but low life expectancy) are the interesting cases."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "model_limitations": [
      {
        "limitation": "Euclidean distance assumes spherical, equally-sized clusters",
        "detail": "K-Means partitions countries into Voronoi cells, forcing roughly convex groups of comparable size. Real economic structures (e.g., oil states vs diversified economies) may be elongated or nested; Gaussian Mixture Models or hierarchical clustering would capture those shapes better. Validate with silhouette scores and consider repeating the analysis with agglomerative clustering as a robustness check."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "K-Means is scale- and initialization-sensitive",
        "detail": "Results depend on the standardization choices, the number of restarts, and the random seed. Always fix random_state and rerun with n_init>=10; report whether cluster membership is stable across seeds."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Feature selection drives the outcome more than the algorithm",
        "detail": "Including CO2_per_capita adds an environmental dimension that partially proxies GDP; including FertilityRate adds a demographic one. Dropping or adding a single indicator can reshuffle clusters. This is measurement-validity risk, not a statistical one: the 'segments' exist only relative to the chosen feature space."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Log transformation linearizes multiplicative relations but distorts levels",
        "detail": "log1p(GDP) compresses the gap between $2,000 and $20,000 much more than between $60,000 and $68,000. Clusters in log-space can separate countries whose absolute income differences are enormous. This is usually desirable for clustering (it dampens whale effects) but must be stated."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "PCA shows a linear projection only",
        "detail": "The 2D scatter can understate separation that exists along the 3rd+ components, and PCA cannot reveal nonlinear structure (e.g., U-shaped relations between inequality and development). Check explained_variance_ratio_; below roughly 65% cumulative variance, the 2D plot is more illustrative than evidential."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Cross-sectional snapshot ignores dynamics",
        "detail": "A single year of data conflates structural differences with cyclical ones: a country in recession (inflation/unemployment spikes) may land in the 'wrong' cluster. A panel extension — clustering on 10-year averages or growth rates — distinguishes level effects from trend effects. Without this, the analysis describes where economies ARE, not where they are GOING."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Data quality and coverage bias",
        "detail": "GDP-per-capita PPP relies on contested purchasing-power parities; Gini indices come from household surveys with heterogeneous methodology; microstates and conflict zones are often missing and were median-imputed, which pulls those countries toward the global middle. Imputation here is not neutral — it manufactures similarity."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Small sample and unit heterogeneity",
        "detail": "N approx 180 with 9 features is a comfortable ratio for clustering but every country is a unit of analysis with huge internal heterogeneity (India contains more economic variety than the EU cluster). Countries are administrative constructs, not economic agents; cluster labels applied to them inherit that arbitrariness."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "No causal interpretation",
        "detail": "Clusters are descriptive associations. Finding that 'low fertility co-occurs with high GDP' says nothing about direction of causality; a segment named 'young low-income' is a stylized fact, not a theory. Any policy recommendation requires an explicit economic model behind the correlations."
      }
    ],
    "deliverables": [
      "Clean, commented Jupyter notebook or script reproducing all six tasks",
      "Elbow plot and silhouette scores with the justified choice of k",
      "Labeled PCA scatter with annotated landmark countries",
      "Final segmentation table: Cluster, name, size, mean indicators, representative countries",
      "Short report section discussing at least four of the model limitations above and one robustness check performed"
    ]
  }
}